WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.959 --> 00:00:04.921
Hi, I am Olivia Wilde, and I'm
 chatting with Laura Kosann.

00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:05.630
Hi.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.216
The 2021 Nicholl Fellow

00:00:08.216 --> 00:00:10.802
for her excellent script,
The Ideal Woman.

00:00:10.802 --> 00:00:12.178
The Ideal Woman takes place

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.764
during the Cuban Missile Crisis
 in American suburbia

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:17.308
and follows a 1960s housewife

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:20.103
who used to be a very famous
 film star in Hollywood,

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:21.688
but whose career was cut short

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.190
 and follows her as
her carefully crafted

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.109
house of cards world
begins to tumble

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:29.029
as she's pitted
between two identities.

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:32.699
Nice to meet you. 

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.367
So nice to meet you. 

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.162
I think they might have told you
 I have an eight week old baby, so-

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:39.289
No one told me that.

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:41.916
that if they're like bags
under my eyes,

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:42.625
Not at all.

00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:44.669
I loved your script. 

00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:46.838
I really, really enjoyed The Ideal Woman.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.009
I find screenwriting to be the most kind
 of confounding part of the process.

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:53.053
And I'm curious,
 where do you find your inspiration?

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:56.639
My writing is very much like this
 process of immersion,

00:00:56.639 --> 00:01:00.185
like with The Ideal Woman,
 you know, the 1960s.

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:03.313
Like, I live there,
 you know, while I'm writing it.

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.190
So I, it's everything I consume. 

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.609
It's everything I read.
It's everything I listen to.

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:10.945
But that step to sort of
 put it onto the script page

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:14.282
is that part that it's like, I'm
 not ready, I'm not ready, I'm not ready.

00:01:14.282 --> 00:01:16.534
And then suddenly when I'm ready.

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:18.369
It just like pours out. 

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:21.372
That’s really interesting it's almost like
 a method acting approach to writing.

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:23.958
It's an interesting interaction 

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:27.796
between screenwriter and director
 when you can sync up.

00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:29.547
It is an amazing feeling.

00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:32.884
But that's why, you know,
you're such a role model to me

00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:33.843
because, like the fact 

00:01:33.843 --> 00:01:37.806
that you direct, I mean, it's
 something that I want to do in the future.

00:01:38.014 --> 00:01:41.476
I don't know. It's just, it's, it's
 so exciting to talk to you.

00:01:42.477 --> 00:01:43.686
Wow, that was bad.

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:45.647
Oh, sorry, guys, 

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:48.358
I just, like, I had to gush
there for a second.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:49.567
With something like
The Ideal Woman.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.945
When you write it,
is the goal to direct it?

00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:53.571
Ideal Woman's one
that I'm always like,

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:56.366
Oh, I would love
to direct this, but I also

00:01:56.366 --> 00:02:01.704
the opportunity to have an
incredible female director, direct

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:04.499
one of my scripts would be such
 an invaluable learning process.

00:02:04.666 --> 00:02:07.794
And especially, as you said, if you have
 someone that kind of speaks your language,

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:11.965
I always wonder what that process
 is like kind of handing over this baby.

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:14.509
And I always wonder if like
actually the writer is like,

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:15.885
”But I could do it better.”

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:18.555
Maybe it’s a little of both.

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:20.181
I think it's probably a little of both.

00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:21.808
What's your pitch process like,

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:25.603
and do you find yourself writing complete
 screenplays and then pitching them?

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:28.690
Or do you pitch ideas first?

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:32.819
I find now it's both
 because I do tend to really love

00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:35.280
to just like, write the screenplay.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.576
And I've been told by producers
 like, “Okay, wait, wait, wait, we don't

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:41.619
we don't need the script just yet.”

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:43.288
I’m like, “No, but I know exactly
 what will happen

00:02:43.288 --> 00:02:46.082
from start to finish, and I can
play out the whole thing for you”

00:02:46.082 --> 00:02:48.084
As a writer, sometimes
you have trouble 

00:02:48.084 --> 00:02:51.796
like dialing it down to
this seed of an idea.

00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:52.714
I was watching you,

00:02:52.714 --> 00:02:54.174
You were talking about Booksmart, 

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.968
and I think you said
 “it's like Training Day for high school”

00:02:57.093 --> 00:02:58.511
I'm not good at that.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:02:59.762
The movie I just directed,

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:02.640
The closest I got was
describing it as 

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.100
”The Feminine Mystique on acid.”

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:06.352
Not really descriptive. 

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.605
Like that doesn't answer
any questions, but for me,

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:10.315
I was like “It makes total sense.”

00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:13.276
My other question was about how you

00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:16.279
how you handle taking notes. 

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:18.281
It is this living, breathing thing.

00:03:18.281 --> 00:03:21.451
And I feel like as a writer, 

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:25.288
you want it to be this thing
 that's like ever-changing in a sense.

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:28.458
Speaking of progress,
you as now a new mother.

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:31.669
How has it shifted your perspective
 on working in this industry?

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.213
It's a challenge, of course, 

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:37.091
and it's so new, by the way, like he's
 literally eight weeks old on Thursday.

00:03:37.091 --> 00:03:40.386
It's crazy to think about, yeah, 

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.680
but I hope that I'm able

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.602
to still be present with my son,
 but also do the same thing.

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:49.812
And I think that it's challenging to do it

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:52.315
while being a mother,
 and it needs to be talked about more.

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:55.026
I does I think, that's why
 I ask it so candidly.

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:57.737
Like, do you have fears
 because it is all possible,

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:01.449
but we have to leave space
 to also air fears.

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:02.283
I have two kids.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.496
I will tell you, like becoming a mother
 makes you much braver because like,

00:04:06.496 --> 00:04:07.747
look what you did eight weeks ago.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.168
I know. Thank you for saying that.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.504
Yeah, you're a goddess and a superhero.

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:17.173
And now you can write anything you want. 

00:04:17.173 --> 00:04:19.217
Thank you so much
 and thank you for your time.

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:21.803
You are a huge inspiration for me.

00:04:21.844 --> 00:04:23.721
I jumped 20 feet
 when I found out

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:25.890
we were chatting, so...

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:26.224
Wow

00:04:26.224 --> 00:04:27.558
and postpartum no less. 

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:29.560
That’s a real, that’s a real feat.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.187
Yeah.

00:04:31.187 --> 00:04:33.314
Okay well, I look forward
to meeting you

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:34.524
 and going to jump back

00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:36.901
into my post process,
 which you totally understand.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:38.403
Thank you so much.

00:04:38.403 --> 00:04:39.195
Bye, everybody.

00:04:40.655 --> 00:04:43.783
Wow. Oh, my.
Did that just happen?

